An unresolved import is a type error ("could not import"), not a structural
failure, so the loader tolerates it rather than failing fatally — the structural
classifier keys fatality on parse errors alone. Here the bad import is not used by
any provider, so generation proceeds normally over the valid ones. (Had the
unresolved type reached the generated output, the core’s invalid-type check would
reject it; see the gen corpus.) The collected type error is asserted via
type-errors.
-- config --
import-path: example.com/app
package-name: app
type-errors: true
-- go.mod --
module example.com/app

go 1.23
-- a.go --
package app

import _ "example.com/does/not/exist"

type T struct{}

//plumb:build
func New() *T { return &T{} }
